            <ArticleTitle>Preincisional infiltration with bupivacaine in tonsillectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>261-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the value of preincisional peritonsillar infiltration of bupivacaine hydrochloride before tonsillectomy under general anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Double-blind trial with two groups comparing 0.25% bupivacaine with placebo (normal saline). The patients were followed up for 10 days after surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Department of Otolaryngology, University Hospital of Aarhus (Denmark).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Men and women 18 to 40 years of age referred to our department for bilateral tonsillectomy, but with no sign of acute infection or suspected malignant neoplasm. Twenty-six patients entered the study; five were excluded for failure to complete the postoperative data; and two, one from each group, had postoperative bleeding. Data from 19 patients were analyzed, nine in the bupivacaine group and 10 in the placebo group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">One group had 5 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine hydrochloride (Marcaine) infiltrated around each tonsil, the other an identical placebo (normal saline) infiltration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">A visual analogue scale was used as the primary efficacy measure. The daily intake of analgesics and a possible weight loss were registered.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A notably lower score on the visual analogue scale was found in the bupivacaine group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Preoperative blocking of nociceptive impulses reduces prolonged postoperative pain, but no reduction in the intake of analgesics was found. Medication was insufficient for the first postoperative days.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
